WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:08.180
As cities balloon with growth, access to nature for people living in urban areas is becoming harder to find.
随着城市迅速扩张，生活在城市地区的人们正越来越难以接触到自然。

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:16.390
If you’re lucky, there might be a pocket park near where you live, but it’s unusual to find places in a city that are relatively wild.
运气好的话，你住的地方附近可能会有一个口袋公园，但在城市里很少能找到相对原生态的地方。

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:27.250
Past research has found health and wellness benefits of nature for humans, but a new study shows that wildness in urban areas is extremely important for human well-being.
过去的研究已经发现自然对人类健康和身心安康有益，但一项新研究表明，城市地区的野性自然对人类福祉极其重要。

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.720
The research team focused on a large urban park.
研究团队以一座大型城市公园为研究对象。

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:40.240
They surveyed several hundred park-goers, asking them to submit a written summary online of a meaningful interaction they had with nature in the park.
他们调查了几百名公园游客，请他们在网上提交一份书面总结，讲述自己在公园里与自然的一次有意义的互动。

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.540
The researchers then examined these submissions, coding experiences into different categories.
研究人员随后仔细查看了这些提交的内容，把各种体验编码，归入不同的类别。

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:57.780
For example, one participant’s experience of “We sat and listened to the waves at the beach for a while” was assigned the categories “sitting at beach” and “listening to waves.”
例如，一位参与者的体验“我们坐在海滩上听了一会儿海浪声”，被归入“坐在海滩上”和“倾听海浪”这两个类别。

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:05.590
Across the 320 submissions, a pattern of categories the researchers call a “nature language” began to emerge.
在全部320份提交内容中，一种类别模式开始显现，研究人员称之为“自然语言”。

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:12.710
After the coding of all submissions, half a dozen categories were noted most often as important to visitors.
在对所有提交内容完成编码后，有六个类别最常被记录为对游客很重要。

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:19.270
These include encountering wildlife, walking along the edge of water, and following an established trail.
其中包括邂逅野生动物、沿水边散步和沿着既有的步道行走。

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:29.320
Naming each nature experience creates a usable language, which helps people recognize and take part in the activities that are most satisfying and meaningful to them.
为每一种自然体验命名，就形成了一种可用的语言，它能帮助人们识别并参与那些最让自己满足、对自己最有意义的活动。

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:38.180
For example, the experience of walking along the edge of water might be satisfying for a young professional on a weekend hike in the park.
例如，对一个周末在公园徒步的年轻上班族来说，沿水边散步的体验可能会令人满足。

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:46.830
Back downtown during a workday, they can enjoy a more domestic form of this interaction by walking along a fountain on their lunch break.
等到工作日回到市中心，这位上班族可以在午休时沿着喷泉散步，以一种更人工化、不那么野性的形式享受这种互动。

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:53.450
“We’re trying to generate a language that helps bring the human-nature interactions back into our daily lives.
“我们正努力创造一种语言，帮助把人与自然的互动带回我们的日常生活。

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:02.280
And for that to happen, we also need to protect nature so that we can interact with it,” said Peter Kahn, a senior author of the study.
而要实现这一点，我们还需要保护自然，这样我们才能与之互动。”该研究的资深作者之一彼得·卡恩说。
